WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.238
Here is a money transfer.

2
00:00:04.238 --> 00:00:08.668
It locks the account it takes money from, then the account it pays into.

3
00:00:08.668 --> 00:00:10.145
Then it moves the money.

4
00:00:10.145 --> 00:00:12.113
Each call is correct on its own.

5
00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:15.175
Now two threads run it at the same time.

6
00:00:15.175 --> 00:00:17.519
Thread 1 moves money from a to b.

7
00:00:17.519 --> 00:00:19.863
Thread 2 moves money from b to a.

8
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:22.807
Thread 1 will lock a, then b.

9
00:00:22.807 --> 00:00:24.947
Thread 2 will lock b, then a.

10
00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:26.923
Thread 1 takes lock a.

11
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:31.176
Before it asks for b, the scheduler switches to thread 2.

12
00:00:31.176 --> 00:00:32.736
Thread 2 takes lock b.

13
00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:35.138
Now thread 1 asks for lock b.

14
00:00:35.138 --> 00:00:37.908
Thread 2 holds it, so thread 1 waits.

15
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.101
Thread 2 asks for lock a.

16
00:00:40.101 --> 00:00:41.990
Thread 1 holds that one.

17
00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:43.721
So thread 2 waits too.

18
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:45.365
Follow the arrows.

19
00:00:45.365 --> 00:00:47.036
Thread 1 waits for b.

20
00:00:47.036 --> 00:00:49.741
Thread 2 holds b, and waits for a.

21
00:00:49.741 --> 00:00:51.093
Thread 1 holds a.

22
00:00:51.093 --> 00:00:53.161
The arrows close a circle.

23
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.726
Each thread lets go of its first lock only after it gets its second.

24
00:00:57.726 --> 00:00:59.635
So neither one will ever move.

25
00:00:59.635 --> 00:01:00.844
That is a deadlock.

26
00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:02.162
Nothing throws.

27
00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:03.112
Nothing logs.

28
00:01:03.112 --> 00:01:11.220
Both threads sit in BLOCKED, CPU use drops to zero, and every request for these accounts queues up behind them.

29
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:16.580
It needs bad timing, so a test that runs one transfer at a time never sees it.

30
00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.203
Under load, it is only a matter of when.

31
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:21.958
This loop is called a wait-for graph.

32
00:01:21.958 --> 00:01:26.871
Deadlock detectors, including the JVM's own, look for exactly this loop.

33
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.971
The fix is one rule that every thread follows.

34
00:01:30.971 --> 00:01:35.512
Give each account a unique key, and always lock the lower key first.

35
00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:39.184
If a has the lower id, both transfers now lock a first.

36
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.943
Thread 1 takes a.

37
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.196
Thread 2 asks for a and waits.

38
00:01:43.196 --> 00:01:45.361
It holds nothing while it waits.

39
00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:46.796
Thread 1 takes b.

40
00:01:46.796 --> 00:01:50.124
It moves the money, and lets go of both locks.

41
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:53.625
Then thread 2 gets a, then b, and finishes too.

42
00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:57.375
Waiting while you hold nothing is plain contention.

43
00:01:57.375 --> 00:01:58.625
No cycle can form.

44
00:01:59.533 --> 00:02:02.983
In code, pick the first and the second lock by id.

45
00:02:02.983 --> 00:02:06.501
Then lock them in that order, in Java or in Kotlin.

46
00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:09.695
The key must be unique and must never change.

47
00:02:09.695 --> 00:02:11.012
An account id works.

48
00:02:11.012 --> 00:02:15.159
hashCode does not, because two different objects can share one.

49
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.102
And the rule must be global.

50
00:02:17.102 --> 00:02:20.811
Put the ordering in one helper, and make every caller use it.
